<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!-- ===========================================================================================
       Folds storage mods' own architect categories into UnifiedStorage.

       Every storage mod adds a category holding its shelves, so running three of them means three
       nearly identical tabs. This collects them.

       <b>Each mod gets its own block, and every block is optional.</b> A player with none of these
       installed gets a UnifiedStorage category with nothing in it, which RimWorld does not draw. A
       player with one gets one tab instead of two. Adding support for another storage mod means
       another block on the same pattern plus its packageId in About.xml's loadAfter.

       <b>Emptied categories are hidden, never removed.</b> Both of these used to be removed once their
       contents had been repointed, on the reasoning that nothing was left naming them. That reasoning
       only covers the mods present when the patch was written. A submod or a third-party patch loaded
       afterwards that names one of these categories finds it gone, which is an unresolved reference and a
       def error in someone else's mod caused by this one. Hiding keeps the reference resolvable.

       <b>Hiding uses this mod's own hidden field</b>, added to DesignationCategoryDef by
       ArchitectCategoryVisibility. Clean Architect did it by naming an unreachable research project as
       the category's researchPrerequisites, and that never worked: an invisible category is not left off
       the architect panel, it is drawn greyed out with a click that answers "Nothing available in
       category". The field takes the category out of the panel's list, which is what actually hides it.

       That older mechanism is still shipped, as ArchitectTabHider, for anything that referenced it.
       Nothing here uses it any more.

       ===========================================================================================
       <b>Each operation is guarded by exactly what it needs, and that is the point of the shape below.</b>

       This file used to test "does the category exist" once and then run both the repoint and the hide
       inside that one branch. It reported a failed patch operation on load, and the reason is worth
       recording because the obvious reading of it is wrong: it was not the case of the storage mod being
       absent. An absent category makes PatchOperationConditional return true, since with no match and no
       nomatch branch it falls through to success.

       What actually happened is that the category existed with nothing left in it. Two separate mods do
       this same fold, and RimWorld applies patches in load order, so whichever runs second finds every
       ThingDef already repointed while the now empty category def is still sitting there. Adaptive
       Storage Framework does it to itself as well: its own patch moves every ASFstorage building to
       LWM_DS_Storage when LWM's Deep Storage is installed. Either way the outer test passed, the inner
       Replace matched nothing, and a Replace that matches nothing fails.

       So the existence of a category no longer implies anything is in it. The repoint is guarded on
       finding a ThingDef to repoint, the hide is guarded on finding a category to hide, and neither
       assumes the other found anything. Nothing here can fail on a load order this mod does not control.
       =========================================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Normal</success>
    <operations>

      <!-- Adaptive Storage Framework -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="ASFstorage"]</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="ASFstorage"]/text()</xpath>
          <value>UnifiedStorage</value>
        </match>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="ASFstorage"]</xpath>

        <!-- Replace or add, rather than always adding. A second hidden element on the same def is not
             an error RimWorld reports; it just parses one of them and drops the other, which is the
             kind of thing that works until it does not. -->
        <match Class="PatchOperationConditional">
          <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="ASFstorage"]/hidden</xpath>
          <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
            <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="ASFstorage"]/hidden</xpath>
            <value>
              <hidden>true</hidden>
            </value>
          </match>
          <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
            <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="ASFstorage"]</xpath>
            <value>
              <hidden>true</hidden>
            </value>
          </nomatch>
        </match>
      </li>

      <!-- LWM's Deep Storage -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="LWM_DS_Storage"]</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="LWM_DS_Storage"]/text()</xpath>
          <value>UnifiedStorage</value>
        </match>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="LWM_DS_Storage"]</xpath>

        <match Class="PatchOperationConditional">
          <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="LWM_DS_Storage"]/hidden</xpath>
          <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
            <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="LWM_DS_Storage"]/hidden</xpath>
            <value>
              <hidden>true</hidden>
            </value>
          </match>
          <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
            <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="LWM_DS_Storage"]</xpath>
            <value>
              <hidden>true</hidden>
            </value>
          </nomatch>
        </match>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
